---
import Slide from '@decks/templates/Slide.astro';

// The model Matt arrived at, placed last because it retro-explains the
// segment rather than setting it up. Everything the panel just watched
// lands in one of these: Code Architecture and Figma Libraries were
// Platform, Accessibility and Documentation were Artifacts, Adoption was
// Service and Go-to-market at once.
//
// Drawn as four stacked bands rather than four columns, because they are
// layers and a stack says so without a caption. Governance is a band
// down the side crossing all four, because that is literally the claim:
// it is not a fifth layer, it is a question asked at every layer.
//
// The four are peers, so they share one treatment. Governance is a
// different kind of thing, so it takes the accent. That is the whole
// colour logic; resist giving each layer its own hue, which reads as
// four unrelated categories.
//
// Hand-drawn in CSS rather than an image, same reasoning as
// 43-ComponentStructure: the labels have to be readable across a room
// and an exported diagram would not scale with the deck.
//
// Two things to say over it, neither of which needs its own slide:
//
//   Every part of every layer has to be removable. A new design tool, a
//   better framework, React to Svelte. We deleted the entire
//   html-sketchapp to Abstract to Sketch pipeline when Figma won, and I
//   would build it again, because manual sync does not scale. The reason
//   that cost a pipeline instead of the system is slide 11: code was the
//   source of truth, so the design tool was replaceable by construction.
//
//   Systems within systems. Teams shipped their own libraries for what
//   was not systemized yet and could change those freely. Core was
//   reviewed. Those libraries were the source material for what got
//   promoted up when it earned it. That IS the governance band: who may
//   change what, at which layer.
//
// And the point of the whole slide, said plainly: most people build the
// first layer and stop.

const layers = [
	{
		name: 'Artifacts',
		items: 'Components, tokens, icons, fonts',
	},
	{
		name: 'Platform',
		items: 'Packaging, versioning, distribution, design-to-code sync, CI',
	},
	{
		name: 'Service',
		items: 'Office hours, design and code review, consults, migration support',
	},
	{
		name: 'Go-to-market',
		items: 'Roadshows, release announcements, training, champions, public roadmap',
	},
];
---

<Slide title="Anvil Design System" subtitle="Four Layers" align="top">
	<div class="grid grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_auto] gap-[28px]">
		<div class="flex flex-col gap-[24px]">
			{
				layers.map((l) => (
					<div class="layer">
						<span class="text-strong text-5xl font-medium">
							{l.name}
						</span>
						<span class="text-subdued text-3xl">{l.items}</span>
					</div>
				))
			}
		</div>

		<div class="governance">
			<span class="gov-label">Governance</span>
		</div>
	</div>
</Slide>

<style>
	.layer {
		display: flex;
		flex-direction: column;
		gap: 12px;
		border-radius: 20px;
		border: 1px solid rgb(156 163 175 / 0.28);
		background: rgb(156 163 175 / 0.07);
		padding: 30px 40px;
	}

	/* One band down the side of all four, not a fifth box beside them.
	   The claim is that governance is a question asked at every layer,
	   so the shape has to cross them rather than sit next to them. */
	.governance {
		display: flex;
		align-items: center;
		justify-content: center;
		width: 148px;
		border-radius: 20px;
		border: 2px dashed rgb(var(--hue) / 0.55);
		background: rgb(var(--hue) / 0.1);
		--hue: 61 223 194;
	}

	/* Rotated so the band reads as a band. Upright text in a 120px column
	   would wrap to three lines and stop looking like one continuous
	   thing crossing the stack. */
	.gov-label {
		writing-mode: vertical-rl;
		transform: rotate(180deg);
		font-size: 36px;
		font-weight: 500;
		letter-spacing: 0.02em;
		color: rgb(var(--hue) / 1);
	}
</style>
